 ## Overview
-The Image-order Rendering Loop is the primary execution structure for direct volume ray casting, iterating over each pixel of the output image to generate a ray and compute its final color contribution [S1, S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of mapping 3D volumetric data to a 2D screen by decoupling pixel processing, allowing for flexible shading modes and adaptive sampling [S1, S2, unofficial]. Unlike object-order methods (rasterization), this approach is described as more "CPU-like" but offers correct perspective projection, single-pass ray casting, and full 32-bit compositing precision without the need for multiple passes or early-z testing [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Ray Traversal and Integration Schemes](8341-ray-traversal-and-integration-schemes.md) by providing the outer control flow that invokes the raymarcher for each pixel.
+The Image-order Rendering Loop is the primary control structure for direct volume ray casting that iterates over output pixels to generate camera rays and invoke a raymarcher for shading [S2, S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of mapping 3D volumetric data to a 2D image by handling ray generation, traversal, and compositing in a single pass per pixel [S1, unofficial]. Unlike object-order methods (rasterization), this approach is described as more "CPU-like" in logic but offers correct perspective projection, adaptive sampling capabilities, and full 32-bit compositing precision without requiring intermediate buffers [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Ray Traversal and Integration Schemes](8341-ray-traversal-and-integration-schemes.md) by providing the outer iteration context for those schemes.
 
 ## How it works
-The method implements a nested loop structure: an outer loop iterates over image pixels, and an inner loop (or function call) performs ray generation, traversal, and compositing.
+The method operates as a nested loop structure: an outer loop over image pixels, an optional loop for multi-sampling anti-aliasing, and an inner call to a raymarching routine that traverses the volume data structure.
 
